<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bituitus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bituitus was a king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> in the late 2nd century BCE, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luernios">Luernios</a>. In 121 BCE he led a huge army of Arverni and Allobroges against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> but was defeated near the junction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> and Isère by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-fabius-maximus">Quintus Fabius Maximus</a>. Later seized by treachery, he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and held captive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bituitus.jpg" alt="Bituitus" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boccace, De Casibus. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bituit_mis_en_prison.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bituitus ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>, a Gaulish people of what is now the Auvergne region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Under his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luernios">Luernius</a> the Arverni had been a powerful opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, and their influence reached into Mediterranean Gaul, the present south of France.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 121 BCE the proconsul Domitius Ahenobarbus made war on the Allobroges, who were allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> under Bituitus. The two peoples were defeated near Vindalium, the present town of Bédarrides, but prepared to fight again, and Bituitus once more took the field with a large army.</p><p>In the autumn of 121 BCE he met the consul Fabius Maximus, grandson of Paullus, where the Isère joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a>. Though greatly outnumbered, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> won a complete victory, and Bituitus's losses were estimated at 120,000. The defeat ended the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> in Mediterranean Gaul. The Allobroges were brought under Roman rule, while the Arverni kept their independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> went on to establish the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis">Gallia Narbonensis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Bituitus was taken prisoner and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, where the senate sentenced him to exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alba-fucens">Alba Fucens</a>, one of three foreign kings known to have been held there. His son Congonnetiacus was also captured and was possibly held with him. He was the last foreign king detained at Alba: later in the Republic such kings were kept at Rome itself, often in the houses of leading officials.</p><p>Fabius was granted a triumph and the name Allobrogicus, and Bituitus was paraded in it wearing his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> ceremonial armour. From the plunder of the Auvergne Fabius built a victory arch, the Fornix Fabianus, on the Via Sacra and set a statue of himself upon it. Bituitus is the last known king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>: later sources, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>'s commentaries on his wars, show them governed by an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a> or council.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bituitus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His army and his son Pharnaces II turned against him, and in 63 BCE at Panticapaeum he died after a failed attempt to poison himself, reportedly killed at his own request by a Gallic officer named <b>Bituitus</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings still ruled elsewhere: <b>Bituitus</b>, king of the Arverni, led an army against Rome in 121 BCE and was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/societies/gaul">Social Hierarchy of Ancient Gaul</a></li><li><blockquote>Their king Luernios was remembered by Greek writers for scattering gold and silver to his followers, and his son <b>Bituitus</b> led a vast army against Rome.</blockquote><a href="/state/arverni">Arverni</a></li></ul></section>
